export const DE_C1_S03_ENGLISH: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "de-die-souffleuse": [
    `For forty-one years Hermine Stadlober had sat in a box barely bigger than a wardrobe: the prompter's box of a Graz theatre, half hidden under the stage, her head just above the boards, the script on her knees. No member of the audience had ever seen her face, and that, she felt, was precisely where the true dignity of her profession lay. A good prompter, her predecessor had explained on her first day, was like a guardian angel: you only noticed her when she wasn't there.`,
    `Tonight was her last evening. They were performing, just as when she had started in 1983, an old Styrian folk play, and for after the show the artistic director had announced a little party in the canteen, with sparkling wine, flowers and, heaven forbid, a speech. Hermine dreaded it. Speeches were texts where nobody was allowed to help you. "Well, Frau Stadlober, last time down in the crypt today?" the stage manager asked in the corridor with a grin. "Mind we don't forget you down there." She smiled politely. Being forgotten was, after all, the art she had practised all her life.`,
    `All of Graz knew the leading man, Konstantin Aigner, from television, and he never missed a chance to tell interviewers that in twenty years he had not once needed a prompter. Indeed, he had never so much as looked in her direction. An actor who ignored the box was like a tightrope walker spitting on the safety net. Fine, let him. She had followed the text for him every evening all the same, word for word, just in case. You never knew. Even stars sometimes fell from the sky, usually in the third act.`,
    `The performance ran like well-oiled clockwork. Twice Hermine whispered a cue to a young actor, who caught it gratefully without letting anything show. Then came the final scene. Aigner, as an old farmer, sat alone at the kitchen table, the farm sold, the children gone. He had only one sentence left to say, the last of the play. He slowly stood up, opened his mouth and said nothing.`,
    `The audience held its breath; it took the silence for art. But Hermine knew this kind of silence. She leaned forward and whispered, clearly and distinctly: "And now let's turn off the light, it's late anyway." Nothing. Aigner didn't move. She repeated the line, louder this time, louder than she had ever dared in forty-one years. Then the great Konstantin Aigner turned round, looked straight into her box for the first time and said, clearly audible to the back row: "Pardon, Frau Stadlober? I'm afraid I didn't catch that."`,
    `A murmur went through the auditorium, then scattered laughter. Six hundred heads turned towards the small, shell-shaped box at the front of the stage. Hermine felt the blood rush to her cheeks. Who did this man think he was? Did he want to make a fool of her as a farewell, in front of everyone, after all these years? She would have liked nothing better than to snap the book shut and disappear, as she always had. But there was only one way out, and it led straight through the embarrassment. She straightened up as far as the box allowed and spoke the line aloud into the hall: "And now let's turn off the light, it's late anyway."`,
    `At that very moment the lights went out. In the darkness, applause broke out such as she had rarely heard. When the spotlights came back on, the whole company was standing at the front of the stage, and before she could protest two technicians pulled her by the hands out of her hole and onto the stage. Aigner pressed an enormous bouquet into her arms. "For forty-one years you've given us our lines," he said quietly, just for her. "We wanted to leave the last one to you. The lighting man was in on it, the director too. Only you weren't."`,
    `Later, in the canteen, the director tapped his glass and asked her to say a few words. Hermine shook her head and raised her glass. "I've already had my line today," she said. "And for once, everyone heard it."`,
  ],
  "de-lob-der-warteschlange": [
    `There are places where people still find themselves, and I don't mean monasteries, alpine huts or silent retreats in the Allgäu. I mean the waiting area of a Cologne citizens' office, Room 0.14: take a number, have a seat, keep calm. Anyone who has once waited three hours there for a new certificate of registration knows what humility is.`,
    `I admit that at first I suffered. I came with a laptop, deadlines and the firm intention of using the time sensibly. After twenty minutes my battery was dead, after forty my patience. The display showed B 217, I was holding B 342, and the numbers moved with the leisureliness of a glacier that has changed its mind. Now and then the display jumped for no apparent reason to A 12, whereupon an elderly gentleman stood up in triumph, as if he had won the lottery.`,
    `But at some point, I can't pin down the moment, something happened to me. I stopped looking at the clock and started looking at people. Next to me sat a midwife from Nippes who had lost her identity card and who told me more about life in an hour than my therapist had in a year. Opposite, a student was knitting a scarf that was a hand's breadth long when I arrived and almost reached the floor by the time my number was called. A man in a trench coat was struggling with a form that had apparently been written in a secret language, and asked the whole room for advice on whether a houseboat counted as a flat. The whole room deliberated. It was, if you like, democracy in its purest form.`,
    `The queue, I learned, is the last truly egalitarian institution in our society. There is no frequent-flyer lounge here, no express counter, no upgrade for a surcharge. The dentist waits next to the delivery driver, the influencer next to the pensioner, and they have all taken the same number from the same grey machine. Before the display board we are all equal, and I venture to say that this idea does more for social peace than many a Sunday speechmaker.`,
    `I became a regular. I registered a change of address, registered back again and applied for a residents' parking permit, although I don't own a car. I brought thermos flasks of coffee and handed out biscuits. The midwife and I arranged to meet, as a matter of course, "at the B row". People greeted each other; people knew each other. When the student finished her scarf there was applause, and a clerk put her head out of Room 0.14 to see what was going on.`,
    `And now this. Since the first of March the city has been giving out appointments exclusively online. You click your way into a calendar, choose a twelve-minute slot and are called on time, with no waiting, no number, no company. The press office speaks of a "milestone in citizen-friendliness". I speak of devastation. Yesterday I went there to collect my new identity card. My appointment was at 9.12 and I was back outside at 9.14. The waiting area was empty; the chairs stood there as if after a funeral.`,
    `People will accuse me of being a nostalgic, an enemy of progress, someone who would like the fax machine back as well. Maybe so. But I ask: where are we supposed to meet now? On the internet, where everyone only meets their own kind? The midwife, by the way, has sent me a message. She has booked an appointment online for a certification she doesn't even need, Thursday at ten, and suggests we have a coffee beforehand. Meeting point: the queue at the bakery opposite. A few refuges remain to us after all.`,
  ],
  "de-das-jahr-ohne-sommer": [
    `14 July 1816. It has now been raining for the ninth day without a break. Half my pupils stay away because they are needed in the fields to save what can no longer be saved; the hay is rotting in the meadows, and the wheat stands black and bent like a funeral procession. The pastor preaches that it is God's punishment for our sins. The chemist, on the other hand, claims it is due to the spots on the sun, which can be observed through a sooted glass. I don't know which of the two I should believe less. One thing is certain: in the Rems valley nobody in living memory has lit the stove in July. I am lighting mine.`,
    `3 August. Snow is said to have fallen on the Alb, the carter tells us, and I am inclined to believe it. The grapes on the slope hang hard and green like peas. My neighbour Jakob Häußler sold his cow yesterday, for a sack of flour and the promise of a second. His wife stood at the fence and watched the animal go until it had disappeared behind the wine press. She didn't say a word. People here get used to silence the way they get used to the weather.`,
    `30 October. The old king is dead. There is much talk of the new one, Wilhelm, and even more of his wife Katharina, a grand duchess from Russia, who is said to be setting up soup kitchens. So far only the news has reached us, not the soup. Bread now costs three times what it did last year. At school I no longer ask the children what they had for breakfast. I have learned that some questions are crueller than any answer.`,
    `2 February 1817. People mix sawdust and ground bark into their bread, and whoever still has potatoes hides them like gold. The Häußlers are selling what they have left and want to go to America by way of Holland. Today little Christian, their eldest and my best pupil, came to say goodbye. He stood awkwardly in the doorway, cap in hand, and said he would write to me from America as soon as he had learned to write in English. I gave him my atlas. He needs it more than I do, for I shall probably never leave this valley.`,
    `6 July. In a newspaper from Mannheim lying about at the Ochsen inn, it says that a Baron von Drais rode through the town on a wooden running machine without a horse, faster than the mail coach. The men at the regulars' table laughed themselves silly over it. I didn't laugh. If oats are so expensive that you can no longer feed a horse, then a man must simply walk himself, and if he can sit on wheels while doing so, all the better. It seems to me that necessity is at times the mother of invention, but reason only rarely its father.`,
    `1 August 1817. Today the first harvest wagon drove into the village, decorated with ribbons and flowers as if for a wedding. The whole parish ran out to meet it, the sexton rang the bells, and there was singing in the church square. Grown men who had not complained all year wept like children. I must confess I was no exception. On Sunday the pastor preached about the grace of God; the chemist talked about the sun. This time I didn't care who was right.`,
    `28 September 1818. In Cannstatt the king has held a great festival for the country people, with horse races and prizes for the finest livestock, so that we remember what we live on. On the same day the messenger brought me a letter from Pennsylvania, in crooked but neat handwriting. Christian writes that they have found land and bought a cow, and that he teaches the younger children of the settlement to read, from my atlas, because there is no other book. He wants to become a schoolmaster. I read the letter three times and then laid it in the chronicle so that it would not get lost.`,
    `What Gottlieb Fritz, schoolmaster in Beutelsbach, could not know: in April 1815 the volcano Tambora had erupted on the distant island of Sumbawa, and its ash had darkened the sky over half of Europe. The festival in Cannstatt is still held every year, now with roller coasters and beer tents. Those who stand on the Wasen today rarely think of the summer that never came, without which this festival would probably not exist.`,
  ],
  "de-was-der-hund-sagt": [
    `For her eleventh birthday Ida Petersen wanted only one thing: the "Bellomat", a collar with a built-in speaker that supposedly used artificial intelligence to translate a dog's barking, growling and whimpering into human language. Her mother Svenja, a lawyer and suspicious by profession, thought the device was expensive nonsense. Her father Malte, a music teacher and enthusiastic by nature, had already ordered it before the discussion had properly begun. Knut, a dachshund mix with melancholy eyes, let the collar be put on him without resistance. For the voice, Ida chose the option "warm, male, mature".`,
    `The family had expected "Hungry!", "Ball!" or at most "I love you!". Instead, on the first evening walk, Knut stopped at the front door, gave a short grunt, and the collar said in a sonorous voice: "I would like to point out that we walked this route yesterday. And the day before. And, in fact, for the last three years." Malte laughed so loudly that a window opened on the ground floor of their old building in Eimsbüttel. Svenja didn't laugh.`,
    `Over the following days Knut proved to be a critic of astonishing range. The dry food, he let them know, tasted like the cardboard box it was delivered in. Malte's evening guitar playing he commented on with a long sigh, which the Bellomat translated as "Please, not Wonderwall again". And when the parents argued in the kitchen one evening about who hadn't emptied the dishwasher, Knut raised his head from his blanket and remarked: "You're not arguing about the dishwasher. You're arguing about who is more tired." After that the kitchen was very quiet.`,
    `Svenja did what she did best: she read the small print. On page fourteen of the terms of use she found the crucial sentence. The translations were based on a language model that continuously analysed the conversations in the household and might therefore reflect "the expectations of the owners rather than the actual inner life of the animal". She read it twice and then slowly put the tablet down on the table. So the dog wasn't speaking for himself at all. He was speaking for them, for the whole family, with the sentences none of them had dared to say. Depending on how you looked at it, that was either a fraud or family therapy at a bargain price.`,
    `She told Malte about it, and Malte, quite against his habit, said nothing for a long time. Then he admitted that he really was tired, had been for months, and that he only kept up his evening guitar playing out of defiance. Svenja admitted that she deliberately didn't empty the dishwasher because she felt she already cleared up enough, at the office and in general. It was the most honest conversation they had had in years, and it took place while Knut lay on the sofa with his ears pricked and didn't say a single word.`,
    `The real test came on a Friday in November. Ida came home from school, threw her rucksack into the corner and sat down on the floor in the hall. She hated school, she said, and everyone in her class, and herself most of all. Her parents stood helplessly in the doorway. Everyone looked at Knut, as if he of all creatures now had to find the right words. Knut trotted over to Ida. The collar beeped twice and announced: "No translation available." Then the dog laid his head on Ida's knee and left it there. Ida buried her face in his fur. "I understood that," she said quietly.`,
    `That evening they took the collar off Knut and put it in the drawer with the old charging cables and the warranty cards. Nobody really missed it. Only Malte still sometimes asks the dog, when they're standing at the front door: "Well, Knut, the same round again?" And Knut looks at him with his melancholy eyes, as if to say that you can know what another being is thinking even without a translation, as long as you walk the same paths together for long enough.`,
  ],
  "de-chiffre-4417": [
    `Ueli Gerber found out at the dairy. He had delivered the milk as he did every morning, and the cheesemaker, a man who otherwise talked at most about the weather and the price of milk, patted him on the shoulder and said: "Congratulations, Ueli. Brave, brave." Only on the way home, when he opened the local paper in the village shop, did he understand. On page eleven, between a used tractor and a belly-dancing course, it said: "Widower, 71, sprightly, own farm in the Emmental, seeks woman for conversation, walks and perhaps more. Replies to box number 4417."`,
    `There was only one person who would dare do such a thing. Corinne, his daughter-in-law, who had taken over the farm with his son and who for three years, ever since Vreni had died, had watched the old man grow more and more silent. "You talk to the cows more than to us," she said when he slammed the paper down on the kitchen table. "At least the cows don't answer back," said Ueli. "Exactly," said Corinne. "That's the problem."`,
    `Two weeks later the postwoman brought a thick envelope from the paper. For four years Margrit Zaugg had ridden up the hill every morning at half past seven on her yellow electric scooter, stopped at the old linden tree by the farm entrance and handed him the post over the fence, usually with a remark about the weather, which he answered with a grunt. That morning she only said: "Lots of post today, Herr Gerber," and rode off without another word. He didn't notice that she had blushed.`,
    `For three days the envelope lay unopened on the sideboard in the living room, like an animal you don't know whether it bites. On the fourth night, when everyone was asleep, Ueli opened it after all. Fourteen letters. A retired teacher from Burgdorf had enclosed a tabular CV and a list of her expectations, including "non-smoker" and "no card-playing evenings". A widow from Thun sent a photo from which she had cut out her late husband, except for his hand, which still lay on her shoulder. One wrote in verse; another wanted to know whether the farm was free of debt. Ueli read everything with growing unease. What had he expected? He was seventy-one and no film star, and a woman who would fall in love with a milking parlour probably didn't exist.`,
    `The last letter consisted of a single sentence, without a name, without a photo, on a squared sheet torn from a notepad: "You have the most beautiful linden tree in the whole valley, and at half past seven the sun stands in it in such a way that one has to stop every time." Ueli read the sentence once, twice, ten times. Then he sat for a long time in the dark living room, thinking. Who came past his linden at half past seven? The bread van came at six, the school bus at ten past seven, the vet only when you called him. And then there was someone else who stopped every morning. He had always thought it was because of the post.`,
    `The next morning at a quarter past seven he was standing under the linden in his good shirt, two cups of coffee in his hands, feeling like an idiot. When the yellow scooter came humming up the hill, he almost hid the cups behind the fence. Margrit stopped, saw the two cups, saw his shirt and switched off the engine. "So you noticed," she said. "I brought the envelope up to you myself," she added, "and the whole way I hoped you wouldn't find my letter. And then I hoped you would." Ueli handed her a cup over the fence. "The coffee's gone cold," he said. "I started waiting too early." It was the longest sentence she had heard from him in four years.`,
    `Corinne saw the two of them from the kitchen window. She said nothing, neither that morning nor at lunch. Only at supper did Ueli clear his throat and say, without looking up from his plate: "You can cancel that advertisement now." Corinne smiled into her soup. "I already have," she said. "It only appeared once."`,
  ],
};

